an></span>","key":"TXWRJdsk8e"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of ","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"zF66kvJAlz"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"0\\le m<M","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le m&lt;M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7804em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">M</span></span></span></span>","key":"G0HCiS1X14"},{"type":"text","value":". Of course, we also have to reverse the order of the columns as we append to each row. Once that is taken care of, the ","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"igO8OBLJUB"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interp2dgrid","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"HRJu5klBCE"},{"type":"text","value":" function doesn’t care that our grid is polar—it just sees a rectangular grid with our Fourier setup in the row dimension and Chebyshev in the column dimension.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"hX1Vo8QuFC"}],"key":"hmhT8HRu2j"}],"icon":false,"class":"dropdown","key":"rlVj5QyOz2"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Program 29","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"p18tHW5SwS"}],"identifier":"program-29","label":"Program 29","html_id":"program-29","implicit":true,"key":"b5246oWHeS"},{"type":"include","file":"SpectralMethodsTrefethen/src/scripts/p29.jl","literal":true,"children":[{"type":"code","value":"using CairoMakie, LaTeXStrings\nusing SpectralMethodsTrefethen\n\"p29 - solve Poisson equation on the unit disk (compare p16 and p28)\"\nfunction p29(N=31, M=40)\n    @assert isodd(N) && iseven(M) \"Must choose odd N and even M\"\n    # Set up r and θ:\n    N2, M2 = div(N - 1, 2), div(M, 2)\n    Dr, r = cheb(N)\n    D²r = Dr^2\n    D₁ = D²r[2:N2+1, 2:N2+1]\n    D₂ = D²r[2:N2+1, N:-1:N2+2]\n    E₁ = Dr[2:N2+1, 2:N2+1]\n    E₂ = Dr[2:N2+1, N:-1:N2+2]\n    θ, _, D²θ = fourier(M)\n\n    # Laplacian in polar coordinates:\n    R = Diagonal(1 ./ r[2:N2+1])\n    J = [zeros(M2, M2) I; I zeros(M2, M2)]\n    Δ = kron(D₁ + R * E₁, I(M)) + kron(D₂ + R * E₂, J) + kron(R^2, D²θ)\n\n    # Right-hand side and solution for u:\n    F = [-r^2 * sin(θ / 2)^4 + sin(6θ) * cos(θ / 2)^2 for θ in θ, r in r[2:N2+1]]\n    v = Δ \\ vec(F)\n\n    # Reshape results, extend to r < 0, and interpolate for the plot:\n    V = reshape(v, M, N2)\n    V = [zeros(M) V]                               # boundary values at r = 1\n    V = [V V[(@. mod1(M2 + (1:M), M)), N2+1:-1:1]]    # extend to r ∈ [-1, 0)\n    r, θ = range(-1, 1, 71), range(0, π, 111)\n    U = interp2dgrid(V, fourinterp, chebinterp, θ, r)\n    X = [r * cos(θ) for θ in θ, r in r]\n    Y = [r * sin(θ) for θ in θ, r in r]\n    fig, ax, plt = contourf(X, Y, U; levels=20, colormap=:amp,\n        axis=(; xlabel=L\"x\", ylabel=L\"y\", aspect=1))\n    Colorbar(fig[1, 2], plt)\n    return fig\nend","lang":"julia","showLineNumbers":true,"label":"p29","identifier":"p29","filename":"p29","html_id":"p29","key":"ZbzZYCzndH"}],"key":"jaHSKkCYFA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are no new algorithmic elements here. Constructing the matrix for the polar Laplacian is the same as in ","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"itSha8VoK4"},{"type":"crossReference","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Program 28","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"grk2cdnvCo"}],"identifier":"p28","label":"p28","kind":"code","template":"Code","resolved":true,"html_id":"p28","key":"cBloRTSD01"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"wdtRWHna3t"}],"key":"XQ8LURkZCn"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output 29","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"m94jAIRZz9"}],"identifier":"output-29","label":"Output 29","html_id":"output-29","implicit":true,"key":"mn2crjmsn1"}],"key":"oVpvqU5mat"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"julia","executable":true,"value":"p29()","identifier":"output29-code","html_id":"output29-code","key":"idqabjDFA5"},{"type":"outputs","id":"8cMOdjWfw6urRtP1vhIk-","children":[{"type":"output","children":[],"jupyter_data":{"output_type":"execute_result","execution_count":3,"metadata":{},"data":{"image/png":{"content_type":"image/png","hash":"7a6b57e2b582e2aedae36b8b9102f9c8","path":"/build/7a6b57e2b582e2aedae36b8b9102f9c8.png"},"text/html":{"content_type":"text/html","hash":"a5edaedb8bcc0a1b6e210badba865751","path":"/build/a5edaedb8bcc0a1b6e